  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 129 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nThe majority of the long term will feature relatively benign weather \nwith a return to more seasonable temperatures and breezy conditions. \nPicking up on Tuesday, this will likely be the last day of above \naverage temperatures as the ridge continues to push eastward away \nfrom the CWA. High temperatures will be in the 50s and 60s which is \nstill anywhere from 10 to 15 degrees above average for mid-November.\n\nA pattern change will arrive on Wednesday as a trough swings into \nthe Rockies. A cold front will move across the area, late Tuesday \nnight or early Wednesday morning, leading to high temperatures in \nthe 40s and 50s--roughly average for this time of year. This front \nwill be mostly dry, with predominately west winds promoting a dry \ndownslope environment. The mountains, however, could see a quick \nshot of snow, with the Sierra Madre Range picking up a few inches of \naccumulation. The Snowys will likely be shadowed and not see much in \nthe way of accumulations. The bigger concern with the frontal \npassage will be strong winds. Marginal high winds will be possible \nas MSLP gradients compress from the front. Winds aloft will increase \nup to about 50 kts with decent subsidence, so cannot rule out 60 MPH \ngusts during the day in the typical southeast Wyoming wind prones.\n\nSeasonal temperatures will stick around on Thursday with little to \nno weather impacts expected. Some lingering breezy conditions are \nstill likely west of the Laramie Range on Thursday, but the rest of \nthe forecast area will see a quiet day. Friday into the weekend, \nthings could get interesting with a potential impactful winter \nstorm. Of course, its still over a week away with plenty of time for \nmodels to come to a solution, but there is a chance for accumulating \nsnow and windy conditions.",
